    <node id="Adaptime Method">
      <data key="d0">Adaptime Method</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">The AdapTime method involves three temporal reasoning actions: reformulate, rewrite, and review, guided by an LLM planner to dynamically execute reasoning steps based on input context.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a92919218c93fbc995ef28388a45a264</data>
      <data key="d4">2604.24175v1</data>
      <data key="d5">1780286811</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="LLM Planner">
      <data key="d0">LLM Planner</data>
      <data key="d1">tool</data>
      <data key="d2">The LLM planner is a crucial component of the AdapTime method that guides the reasoning process by selecting appropriate temporal reasoning actions for each question type.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a92919218c93fbc995ef28388a45a264</data>
      <data key="d4">2604.24175v1</data>
      <data key="d5">1780286811</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Temporal Information">
      <data key="d0">Temporal Information</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Temporal information is a critical aspect of understanding and processing natural language, which current large language models often fail to handle effectively.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a92919218c93fbc995ef28388a45a264</data>
      <data key="d4">2604.24175v1</data>
      <data key="d5">1780286817</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Temporal Reasoning Actions">
      <data key="d0">Temporal Reasoning Actions</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Temporal reasoning actions refer to specific steps taken in processing and understanding temporal information, including reformulate, rewrite, and review.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a92919218c93fbc995ef28388a45a264</data>
      <data key="d4">2604.24175v1</data>
      <data key="d5">1780286822</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Temporal Reasoning Capabilities">
      <data key="d0">Temporal Reasoning Capabilities</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Temporal reasoning capabilities refer to a model's ability to understand and process temporal information.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a92919218c93fbc995ef28388a45a264</data>
      <data key="d4">2604.24175v1</data>
      <data key="d5">1780286822</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Aleatoric Uncertainty">
      <data key="d0">Aleatoric Uncertainty</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Aleatoric uncertainty refers to irreducible input ambiguity and is estimated via a dedicated ambiguity output trained to match annotator disagreement when available in CREDENCE.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-186325b8d21aab395207850cb3577223</data>
      <data key="d4">2604.24170v1</data>
      <data key="d5">1780287105</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Concept Bottleneck Models">
      <data key="d0">Concept Bottleneck Models</data>
      <data key="d1">model</data>
      <data key="d2">Concept Bottleneck Models typically output point concept probabilities that conflate epistemic uncertainty with aleatoric uncertainty, making concept-level uncertainty hard to interpret and act upon.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-186325b8d21aab395207850cb3577223</data>
      <data key="d4">2604.24170v1</data>
      <data key="d5">1780287105</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Credence">
      <data key="d0">Credence</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">CREDENCE is a CBM framework that decomposes concept uncertainty by construction and represents each concept as a credal prediction (a probability interval).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-186325b8d21aab395207850cb3577223</data>
      <data key="d4">2604.24170v1</data>
      <data key="d5">1780287112</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Epistemic Uncertainty">
      <data key="d0">Epistemic Uncertainty</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Epistemic uncertainty refers to reducible model underspecification, which can be derived from disagreement across diverse concept heads in CREDENCE.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-186325b8d21aab395207850cb3577223</data>
      <data key="d4">2604.24170v1</data>
      <data key="d5">1780287112</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Annotator Disagreement">
      <data key="d0">Annotator Disagreement</data>
      <data key="d1">metric</data>
      <data key="d2">Annotator disagreement closely tracks aleatoric uncertainty in CREDENCE.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-186325b8d21aab395207850cb3577223</data>
      <data key="d4">2604.24170v1</data>
      <data key="d5">1780287116</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Concept Heads">
      <data key="d0">Concept Heads</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Concept heads are used to derive epistemic uncertainty from disagreement across diverse concept heads in CREDENCE.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-186325b8d21aab395207850cb3577223</data>
      <data key="d4">2604.24170v1</data>
      <data key="d5">1780287116</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Model Underspecification">
      <data key="d0">Model Underspecification</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Model underspecification refers to reducible model uncertainty in CREDENCE.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-186325b8d21aab395207850cb3577223</data>
      <data key="d4">2604.24170v1</data>
      <data key="d5">1780287120</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Prediction Errors">
      <data key="d0">Prediction Errors</data>
      <data key="d1">metric</data>
      <data key="d2">Prediction errors are positively associated with epistemic uncertainty in CREDENCE.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-186325b8d21aab395207850cb3577223</data>
      <data key="d4">2604.24170v1</data>
      <data key="d5">1780287120</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Probability Interval">
      <data key="d0">Probability Interval</data>
