      <data key="d4">2604.23195v1</data>
      <data key="d5">1780355492</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Spice Netlists">
      <data key="d0">Spice Netlists</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">SPICE netlists are a type of heterogeneous representation used in analog circuit design.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-24290bb35376401e34d3a4168d44a3d4</data>
      <data key="d4">2604.23195v1</data>
      <data key="d5">1780355497</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Two-Stage Repair Pipeline">
      <data key="d0">Two-Stage Repair Pipeline</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">The Two-Stage Repair Pipeline is a technique used to build AnalogRetriever on top of Masala-CHAI dataset.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-24290bb35376401e34d3a4168d44a3d4</data>
      <data key="d4">2604.23195v1</data>
      <data key="d5">1780355497</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Adaplan-H">
      <data key="d0">Adaplan-H</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">AdaPlan-H is a self-adaptive hierarchical planning mechanism that mimics human planning strategies by initiating with a coarse-grained macro plan and progressively refining it based on task complexity.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ce939ac16237a67e4084c40760f08ec3</data>
      <data key="d4">2604.23194v1</data>
      <data key="d5">1780355672</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="LLM Agents">
      <data key="d0">LLM Agents</data>
      <data key="d1">application</data>
      <data key="d2">LLM agents are powerful approaches for solving dynamic and multi-step tasks, employing planning mechanisms to guide long-term actions in dynamic environments.&lt;SEP&gt;LLM Agents are language models that can be vulnerable to skill poisoning attacks.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ce939ac16237a67e4084c40760f08ec3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7f1b8d88def5ad634b9cb09c9574686b</data>
      <data key="d4">2604.23194v1&lt;SEP&gt;2604.22888v1</data>
      <data key="d5">1780384360</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Progressive Refinement">
      <data key="d0">Progressive Refinement</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Progressive refinement is a principle in cognitive science that inspires AdaPlan-H's self-adaptive hierarchical planning mechanism.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ce939ac16237a67e4084c40760f08ec3</data>
      <data key="d4">2604.23194v1</data>
      <data key="d5">1780355679</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Task Execution Success Rates">
      <data key="d0">Task Execution Success Rates</data>
      <data key="d1">metric</data>
      <data key="d2">Task execution success rates are improved significantly using AdaPlan-H, while mitigating overplanning at the planning level.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ce939ac16237a67e4084c40760f08ec3</data>
      <data key="d4">2604.23194v1</data>
      <data key="d5">1780355679</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Cognitive Science">
      <data key="d0">Cognitive Science</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Cognitive science is a field of study that inspired AdaPlan-H's self-adaptive hierarchical planning mechanism.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ce939ac16237a67e4084c40760f08ec3</data>
      <data key="d4">2604.23194v1</data>
      <data key="d5">1780355683</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Environment Setup Success Rate (Essr)">
      <data key="d0">Environment Setup Success Rate (Essr)</data>
      <data key="d1">metric</data>
      <data key="d2">ESSR measures the success rate of environment setup for software engineering tasks.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-24860834f8b0f49fecc8536d9040fcfa</data>
      <data key="d4">2604.23190v1</data>
      <data key="d5">1780355840</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Rat">
      <data key="d0">Rat</data>
      <data key="d1">model</data>
      <data key="d2">RAT is a language-agnostic framework for automated environment configuration on arbitrary repositories.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-24860834f8b0f49fecc8536d9040fcfa</data>
      <data key="d4">2604.23190v1</data>
      <data key="d5">1780355840</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Ratbench">
      <data key="d0">Ratbench</data>
      <data key="d1">benchmark</data>
      <data key="d2">RATBench is a benchmark that reflects the distribution and heterogeneity of real-world repositories, used to evaluate RAT's performance.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-24860834f8b0f49fecc8536d9040fcfa</data>
      <data key="d4">2604.23190v1</data>
      <data key="d5">1780355846</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Planning Mechanism">
      <data key="d0">Planning Mechanism</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">The planning mechanism in RAT's pipeline enables the selection of tools and configurations based on repository requirements.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-24860834f8b0f49fecc8536d9040fcfa</data>
      <data key="d4">2604.23190v1</data>
      <data key="d5">1780355846</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Robust Sandbox">
      <data key="d0">Robust Sandbox</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The robust sandbox in RAT's pipeline provides a secure environment for testing and validating configurations without affecting the actual repository.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-24860834f8b0f49fecc8536d9040fcfa</data>
      <data key="d4">2604.23190v1</data>
      <data key="d5">1780355851</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Semantic Initialization">
      <data key="d0">Semantic Initialization</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Semantic initialization is a stage in RAT's pipeline that integrates semantic information for environment configuration.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-24860834f8b0f49fecc8536d9040fcfa</data>
      <data key="d4">2604.23190v1</data>
      <data key="d5">1780355851</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Specialized Toolset">
      <data key="d0">Specialized Toolset</data>
      <data key="d1">tool</data>
      <data key="d2">RAT features a specialized toolset that supports various programming languages and environments for environment configuration.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-24860834f8b0f49fecc8536d9040fcfa</data>
      <data key="d4">2604.23190v1</data>
      <data key="d5">1780355855</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Abdominal Hernia">
      <data key="d0">Abdominal Hernia</data>
      <data key="d1">condition</data>
      <data key="d2">Abdominal hernias are a condition associated with the DyABD dataset, characterized by high recurrence rates.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-939591faf029ffb75f2b84f8ba7aa53b</data>
      <data key="d4">2604.23187v1</data>
      <data key="d5">1780356136</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Abdominal Muscle Segmentation Task">
      <data key="d0">Abdominal Muscle Segmentation Task</data>
      <data key="d1">task</data>
      <data key="d2">The Abdominal Muscle Segmentation Task is the first proposed task in DyABD, requiring segmentation of abdominal muscles in dynamic MRIs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-939591faf029ffb75f2b84f8ba7aa53b</data>
      <data key="d4">2604.23187v1</data>
      <data key="d5">1780356136</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Dice Coefficient">
      <data key="d0">Dice Coefficient</data>
      <data key="d1">metric</data>
      <data key="d2">The Dice Coefficient is a metric used to evaluate segmentation model performance on DyABD, achieving 0.82 in this work.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-939591faf029ffb75f2b84f8ba7aa53b</data>
      <data key="d4">2604.23187v1</data>
      <data key="d5">1780356143</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Dyabd">
      <data key="d0">Dyabd</data>
      <data key="d1">dataset</data>
      <data key="d2">DyABD is a novel benchmark dataset of dynamic abdominal MRIs from patients with abdominal hernias, including high-quality abdominal muscle annotations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-939591faf029ffb75f2b84f8ba7aa53b</data>
      <data key="d4">2604.23187v1</data>
      <data key="d5">1780356143</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Dynamic Mri">
      <data key="d0">Dynamic Mri</data>
      <data key="d1">dataset</data>
      <data key="d2">Dynamic MRI is a type of imaging used in DyABD to capture anatomical variability during patient exercises.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-939591faf029ffb75f2b84f8ba7aa53b</data>
      <data key="d4">2604.23187v1</data>
      <data key="d5">1780356147</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Few Shot Paradigm">
      <data key="d0">Few Shot Paradigm</data>
      <data key="d1">paradigm</data>
      <data key="d2">The Few Shot paradigm involves training models on a small amount of labeled data and applying them to new, unseen data like DyABD.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-939591faf029ffb75f2b84f8ba7aa53b</data>
      <data key="d4">2604.23187v1</data>
      <data key="d5">1780356147</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Supervised Paradigm">
      <data key="d0">Supervised Paradigm</data>
      <data key="d1">paradigm</data>
      <data key="d2">Supervised learning paradigm involves training models on labeled data and applying them to new, unseen data like DyABD.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-939591faf029ffb75f2b84f8ba7aa53b</data>
      <data key="d4">2604.23187v1</data>
      <data key="d5">1780356152</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Zero Shot Paradigm">
      <data key="d0">Zero Shot Paradigm</data>
      <data key="d1">paradigm</data>
      <data key="d2">The Zero Shot paradigm involves training models without any labeled data and applying them to new, unseen data like DyABD.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-939591faf029ffb75f2b84f8ba7aa53b</data>
      <data key="d4">2604.23187v1</data>
      <data key="d5">1780356152</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Clinical Research">
      <data key="d0">Clinical Research</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Clinical research is a concept promoted by DyABD to investigate the causes and treatments of abdominal hernias.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-939591faf029ffb75f2b84f8ba7aa53b</data>
      <data key="d4">2604.23187v1</data>
      <data key="d5">1780356156</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="High Recurrence Rates">
      <data key="d0">High Recurrence Rates</data>
      <data key="d1">condition</data>
      <data key="d2">High recurrence rates are a characteristic of abdominal hernias associated with DyABD.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-939591faf029ffb75f2b84f8ba7aa53b</data>
      <data key="d4">2604.23187v1</data>
      <data key="d5">1780356156</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Medical Image Segmentation">
      <data key="d0">Medical Image Segmentation</data>
      <data key="d1">task</data>
      <data key="d2">Medical image segmentation is a task that involves segmenting medical images to identify specific structures or features.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-939591faf029ffb75f2b84f8ba7aa53b</data>
      <data key="d4">2604.23187v1</data>
      <data key="d5">1780356160</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Progress In Medical Image Segmentation">
      <data key="d0">Progress In Medical Image Segmentation</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Progress in medical image segmentation is a concept redefined by DyABD based on its evaluation of existing techniques.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-939591faf029ffb75f2b84f8ba7aa53b</data>
      <data key="d4">2604.23187v1</data>
      <data key="d5">1780356160</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Escalation Criteria Framework">
      <data key="d0">Escalation Criteria Framework</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Escalation Criteria Framework is a proposed common reference point for determining when an AI incident warrants international coordination.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d65181108619295f7f28b07aeb2cb920</data>
      <data key="d4">2604.23183v1</data>
      <data key="d5">1780356533</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Gpai Code Of Practice">
      <data key="d0">Gpai Code Of Practice</data>
      <data key="d1">guideline</data>
      <data key="d2">The GPAI Code of Practice provides guidelines for responsible AI development and incident response, including escalation criteria.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d65181108619295f7f28b07aeb2cb920</data>
      <data key="d4">2604.23183v1</data>
      <data key="d5">1780356540</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Model Weight Exfiltration Risk Detection">
      <data key="d0">Model Weight Exfiltration Risk Detection</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Model weight exfiltration risk detection is a criterion for assessing whether an incident warrants escalation, focusing on severe and irreversible harm.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d65181108619295f7f28b07aeb2cb920</data>
      <data key="d4">2604.23183v1</data>
      <data key="d5">1780356540</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Quantitatively Testable Thresholds">
      <data key="d0">Quantitatively Testable Thresholds</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Quantitatively testable thresholds are criteria for assessing whether an incident warrants escalation, focusing on measurable and practical terms.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d65181108619295f7f28b07aeb2cb920</data>
      <data key="d4">2604.23183v1</data>
      <data key="d5">1780356544</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Responsible Actor Data Availability">
      <data key="d0">Responsible Actor Data Availability</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The responsible actor's data availability is a criterion for assessing whether an incident warrants escalation, focusing on the quality and quantity of available data.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d65181108619295f7f28b07aeb2cb920</data>
      <data key="d4">2604.23183v1</data>
      <data key="d5">1780356544</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Sb 53">
      <data key="d0">Sb 53</data>
      <data key="d1">regulation</data>
      <data key="d2">SB 53 is a regulatory framework that guides the assessment of AI incidents and their potential impact on national handling.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d65181108619295f7f28b07aeb2cb920</data>
      <data key="d4">2604.23183v1</data>
      <data key="d5">1780356548</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Systemic Harms Accumulation Risk">
      <data key="d0">Systemic Harms Accumulation Risk</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Systemic harms accumulation risk is a criterion for assessing whether an incident warrants escalation, focusing on the potential impact of accumulated risks.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d65181108619295f7f28b07aeb2cb920</data>
      <data key="d4">2604.23183v1</data>
      <data key="d5">1780356548</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="International AI Incident Response">
      <data key="d0">International AI Incident Response</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">International AI incident response refers to the coordinated effort among jurisdictions to address AI-related incidents that require global attention.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d65181108619295f7f28b07aeb2cb920</data>
      <data key="d4">2604.23183v1</data>
      <data key="d5">1780356552</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Model Developers">
      <data key="d0">Model Developers</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Model developers are responsible for developing and deploying AI models, which may be subject to regulatory frameworks and guidelines for incident response.&lt;SEP&gt;Model developers create and refine AI models used in hiring applications, often with proprietary configurations that hinder integrated evaluation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d65181108619295f7f28b07aeb2cb920&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8a9bb4320aa0f6b0b69b9c0e565dafeb</data>
      <data key="d4">2604.23183v1&lt;SEP&gt;2604.22679v1</data>
      <data key="d5">1780373494</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="National Handling">
      <data key="d0">National Handling</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">National handling refers to the individual country's approach to addressing AI incidents within their jurisdiction, which may involve escalation to international coordination.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d65181108619295f7f28b07aeb2cb920</data>
      <data key="d4">2604.23183v1</data>
      <data key="d5">1780356557</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Regulatory Frameworks">
      <data key="d0">Regulatory Frameworks</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Regulatory frameworks refer to the guidelines and regulations that govern AI development and deployment, including those related to incident response.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d65181108619295f7f28b07aeb2cb920</data>
      <data key="d4">2604.23183v1</data>
      <data key="d5">1780356557</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Systemic Harms">
      <data key="d0">Systemic Harms</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Systemic harms refer to the potential long-term consequences of AI incidents that may not be immediately apparent, such as accumulation risks.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d65181108619295f7f28b07aeb2cb920</data>
      <data key="d4">2604.23183v1</data>
      <data key="d5">1780356561</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Time Pressure">
      <data key="d0">Time Pressure</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Time pressure refers to the urgency and time constraints under which AI incidents may need to be addressed, particularly when thresholds for escalation are set.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d65181108619295f7f28b07aeb2cb920</data>
      <data key="d4">2604.23183v1</data>
      <data key="d5">1780356561</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Classical Coverage">
      <data key="d0">Classical Coverage</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Classical Coverage is a baseline method that relies on coverage objectives for multi-robot monitoring and active perception approaches.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e3c7bf6aa95dbbb47a5109f81b1a609e</data>
      <data key="d4">2604.23179v1</data>
      <data key="d5">1780356857</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Cooperative Informative Sensing">
      <data key="d0">Cooperative Informative Sensing</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Cooperative Informative Sensing is a framework for cooperative active observation in monitoring dynamic indoor environments via multi-agent reinforcement learning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e3c7bf6aa95dbbb47a5109f81b1a609e</data>
      <data key="d4">2604.23179v1</data>
      <data key="d5">1780356857</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Decentralized Control Problem">
      <data key="d0">Decentralized Control Problem</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Decentralized Control Problem is a problem formulation for cooperative active observation where multiple robots adjust their motion to directly optimize monitoring accuracy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e3c7bf6aa95dbbb47a5109f81b1a609e</data>
      <data key="d4">2604.23179v1</data>
      <data key="d5">1780356863</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
