    <node id="16-Domain Stream">
      <data key="d0">16-Domain Stream</data>
      <data key="d1">dataset</data>
      <data key="d2">The 16-domain stream is a larger-scale dataset consisting of sixteen tasks, used to evaluate the performance of adaptive decoupled moment routing in more challenging continual learning scenarios.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-350265fa6cfa6c36f900b8cfb64b92be</data>
      <data key="d4">2604.22407v1</data>
      <data key="d5">1780383459</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="8-Domain Continual Lm">
      <data key="d0">8-Domain Continual Lm</data>
      <data key="d1">dataset</data>
      <data key="d2">The 8-domain continual learning dataset consists of eight tasks with high overlap, used for evaluating the performance of gradient modification methods in continual learning scenarios.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-350265fa6cfa6c36f900b8cfb64b92be</data>
      <data key="d4">2604.22407v1</data>
      <data key="d5">1780383459</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Adam">
      <data key="d0">Adam</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Adam is a popular stochastic gradient descent (SGD) optimizer that uses momentum and second-moment estimates for adaptive learning rate adjustment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-350265fa6cfa6c36f900b8cfb64b92be</data>
      <data key="d4">2604.22407v1</data>
      <data key="d5">1780383466</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Adaptive Decoupled Moment Routing">
      <data key="d0">Adaptive Decoupled Moment Routing</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Adaptive decoupled moment routing is an extension of decoupled moment routing that adapts to the overlap between tasks or domains.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-350265fa6cfa6c36f900b8cfb64b92be</data>
      <data key="d4">2604.22407v1</data>
      <data key="d5">1780383466</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Continual Learning">
      <data key="d0">Continual Learning</data>
      <data key="d1">task</data>
      <data key="d2">Continual learning involves training models on sequential, non-i.i.d. data streams where the model must adapt to new tasks or domains without catastrophic forgetting of previously learned knowledge.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-350265fa6cfa6c36f900b8cfb64b92be</data>
      <data key="d4">2604.22407v1</data>
      <data key="d5">1780383470</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Decoupled Moment Routing">
      <data key="d0">Decoupled Moment Routing</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Decoupled moment routing is a technique that separates the first and second moments of the gradient update, allowing for more stable learning in continual learning scenarios.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-350265fa6cfa6c36f900b8cfb64b92be</data>
      <data key="d4">2604.22407v1</data>
      <data key="d5">1780383470</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Gradient Modification">
      <data key="d0">Gradient Modification</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Gradient modification refers to techniques that alter the gradient updates during training, such as projection, penalty rescaling, and replay mixing.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-350265fa6cfa6c36f900b8cfb64b92be</data>
      <data key="d4">2604.22407v1</data>
      <data key="d5">1780383474</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Hidden Failure Modes Of Gradient Modification Under Adam In Continual Learning">
      <data key="d0">Hidden Failure Modes Of Gradient Modification Under Adam In Continual Learning</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Hidden failure modes refer to a previously unidentified issue where gradient modification methods fail to perform well when combined with the Adam optimizer in continual learning scenarios.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-350265fa6cfa6c36f900b8cfb64b92be</data>
      <data key="d4">2604.22407v1</data>
      <data key="d5">1780383474</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Overlap-Aware Adaptive Strength">
      <data key="d0">Overlap-Aware Adaptive Strength</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Overlap-aware adaptive strength refers to the ability of an algorithm to adapt its strength based on the overlap between tasks or domains, which can help improve the model's performance on sequential tasks.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-350265fa6cfa6c36f900b8cfb64b92be</data>
      <data key="d4">2604.22407v1</data>
      <data key="d5">1780383479</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Penalty Rescaling">
      <data key="d0">Penalty Rescaling</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Penalty rescaling is a technique that modifies the gradients during training by scaling down the penalty term, which can help improve the model's performance on sequential tasks.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-350265fa6cfa6c36f900b8cfb64b92be</data>
      <data key="d4">2604.22407v1</data>
      <data key="d5">1780383483</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Replay Mixing">
      <data key="d0">Replay Mixing</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Replay mixing is a technique that combines the gradients from different tasks during training by mixing them together, which can help improve the model's performance on sequential tasks.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-350265fa6cfa6c36f900b8cfb64b92be</data>
      <data key="d4">2604.22407v1</data>
      <data key="d5">1780383483</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Vanilla Forgetting">
      <data key="d0">Vanilla Forgetting</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Vanilla forgetting refers to the baseline performance of a model when trained on sequential tasks without any adaptation or regularization techniques.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-350265fa6cfa6c36f900b8cfb64b92be</data>
      <data key="d4">2604.22407v1</data>
      <data key="d5">1780383487</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Equal-Quality Pairs">
      <data key="d0">Equal-Quality Pairs</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Equal-quality pairs are constructed to have negligible quality differences, enabling statistical disentanglement of discriminability from bias propensity without human gold standards.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-879cb5db2211c2283dd060ddcd2850ac</data>
      <data key="d4">2604.22891v1</data>
      <data key="d5">1780384159</data>
      <data key="d6" />
