    </edge>
    <edge source="Abpms Process Frame" target="Framed Autonomy">
      <data key="d7">1.0</data>
      <data key="d8">Framed autonomy emerges from the ABPMS process frame's hybrid representation and enables (semi) autonomous behavior.</data>
      <data key="d9">(semi) autonomous behavior,hybrid representation</data>
      <data key="d10">chunk-38034d43cb3b9bf0f3f58267ae12607f</data>
      <data key="d11">2604.22455v1</data>
      <data key="d12">1780380178</data>
      <data key="d13" />
    </edge>
    <edge source="Abpms Process Frame" target="Declarative Paradigm">
      <data key="d7">1.0</data>
      <data key="d8">The ABPMS process frame incorporates heterogeneous knowledge from declarative paradigms and adopts an open-world assumption.</data>
      <data key="d9">assumption,hybrid representation</data>
      <data key="d10">chunk-38034d43cb3b9bf0f3f58267ae12607f</data>
      <data key="d11">2604.22455v1</data>
      <data key="d12">1780380182</data>
      <data key="d13" />
    </edge>
    <edge source="Abpms Process Frame" target="Declare">
      <data key="d7">2.0</data>
      <data key="d8">The Declare language is used to map subsets of discovered declarative constraints into equivalent semi-concurrently executed procedural fragments.&lt;SEP&gt;The ABPMS process frame incorporates heterogeneous knowledge and uses the Declare language to map subsets of discovered declarative constraints into equivalent semi-concurrently executed procedural fragments.</data>
      <data key="d9">hybrid representation,tool</data>
      <data key="d10">chunk-38034d43cb3b9bf0f3f58267ae12607f</data>
      <data key="d11">2604.22455v1</data>
      <data key="d12">1780380186</data>
      <data key="d13" />
    </edge>
    <edge source="Constraint-Like Interpretation" target="Declare">
      <data key="d7">1.0</data>
      <data key="d8">The Declare language is based on the constraint-like interpretation of procedural process models.</data>
      <data key="d9">concept,tool</data>
      <data key="d10">chunk-38034d43cb3b9bf0f3f58267ae12607f</data>
      <data key="d11">2604.22455v1</data>
      <data key="d12">1780380182</data>
      <data key="d13" />
    </edge>
    <edge source="Constraint-Like Interpretation" target="Procedural Process Models">
      <data key="d7">1.0</data>
      <data key="d8">Procedural process models are interpreted as constraints on activities within that model, without imposing explicit execution requirements or limitations.</data>
      <data key="d9">execution semantics,interpretation</data>
      <data key="d10">chunk-38034d43cb3b9bf0f3f58267ae12607f</data>
      <data key="d11">2604.22455v1</data>
      <data key="d12">1780380186</data>
      <data key="d13" />
    </edge>
    <edge source="Declarative Paradigm" target="Procedural Process Models">
      <data key="d7">2.0</data>
      <data key="d8">The declarative paradigm assumes that procedural process models should also adopt an open-world assumption and a constraint-like interpretation.&lt;SEP&gt;Procedural process models are interpreted as constraints on activities within that model, adopting an open-world assumption from the declarative paradigm.</data>
      <data key="d9">assumption,interpretation</data>
      <data key="d10">chunk-38034d43cb3b9bf0f3f58267ae12607f</data>
      <data key="d11">2604.22455v1</data>
      <data key="d12">1780380178</data>
      <data key="d13" />
    </edge>
    <edge source="Declarative Paradigm" target="Open-World Assumption">
      <data key="d7">1.0</data>
      <data key="d8">The open-world assumption is a fundamental concept in the declarative paradigm, which assumes that knowledge is represented as a set of facts and rules.</data>
      <data key="d9">assumption,paradigm</data>
      <data key="d10">chunk-38034d43cb3b9bf0f3f58267ae12607f</data>
      <data key="d11">2604.22455v1</data>
      <data key="d12">1780380191</data>
      <data key="d13" />
    </edge>
    <edge source="Agent Society" target="Probing Agents">
      <data key="d7">4.0</data>
      <data key="d8">Probing Agents interact with the Agent Society to evaluate collective intelligence across three tiers: joint reasoning, information synthesis, and basic interaction.&lt;SEP&gt;The interaction between Probing Agents and the Agent Society is limited by Shallow Interaction, preventing agents from exchanging information and building on each other's outputs.&lt;SEP&gt;The evaluation of the Agent Society by Probing Agents reveals a stark absence of collective intelligence in performing Complex Reasoning Tasks.&lt;SEP&gt;The interaction between Probing Agents and the Agent Society is evaluated for its ability to perform Trivial Coordination Tasks.</data>
      <data key="d9">controlled entities,evaluation,interaction,limitation</data>
      <data key="d10">chunk-121ca295f22e17da9a2b408d052d49b7</data>
      <data key="d11">2604.22452v1</data>
      <data key="d12">1780380424</data>
      <data key="d13" />
    </edge>
    <edge source="Agent Society" target="Collective Intelligence">
